1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,160
Hi everyone, my name is Mike. I'm from the Burp Suite desktop development team and today I'm here

2
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:14,160
to talk to you about the new tool we've recently added to Burp Suite called Organizer. So we've

3
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:18,720
been speaking to users and one of the recent pain points we identified was that people were having

4
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:23,360
trouble collating interesting requests and responses that they found while pen testing

5
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,920
and evaluating web applications. Let me jump into Burp Suite and I can show you what I mean.

6
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:35,440
So that's a good well-timed automatic backup. So here I am on the dashboard. If I move over to the

7
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:41,280
Repeater tab you can see that I've tried to collate all the interesting requests with their responses

8
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:47,120
in Repeater using Repeater tabs. Now this is okay, it works kind of well, but the issue is that we're

9
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:53,120
not able to filter, search or sort these results and we're not able to accompany it with any free

10
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:57,200
text data. So if I find anything out at the time I'm not able to store that alongside the request

11
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:03,840
and responses which I can then come back to later to help jog my memory. So if I jump over to Organizer

12
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:09,040
which is a new tool on the top level tab, we can see that it's currently empty. So let's add some

13
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:15,520
things to it. You can add requests and their responses to Organizer using the Send to Organizer

14
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:21,600
feature which you can find in the context menu or you can use the Send to Organizer hotkey which is

15
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:30,000
mapped to Command O on Mac and maybe Control O in Windows and Linux. So if I send a few requests over

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,280
with their responses, we can see that they've entered the Organizer table with the new status.

17
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:42,800
Now the Status column is a new feature in Virp that allows you to keep track of where this request is up

18
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:47,360
to in your testing workflow. So if you're not interested in it anymore. Furthermore, if you don't

19
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:53,520
want to see it anymore, you can delete using the context menu. Once you select one of the entries in

20
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:57,920
Organizer, you can see the request and response in the editors below and you can also see that we've

21
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:04,000
added a new notes free text editor here. So you can add any additional information that you want.

22
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:13,440
For example, hello. So that data will then be stored alongside your request and response in your project

23
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:18,000
file. So the next time you open Virp, all of that will be available to you even in six months time

24
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,640
when you've forgotten completely about where you found this and what it meant. Similar to other places

25
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:30,960
in Virp, we have filter and sorting functionality being a table and we also have a free text search

26
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:35,600
field which search the request, response and notes if you're looking for something specific.

27
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:42,800
Finally, if I jump back to Repeater, we've also added the new notes pane to the sidebar in the

28
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:47,840
Repeater tool. So as you're manually evaluating a website, you found something interesting. You can

